    "question": {
      ":question/id": "25093981-29f4-57e5-9faf-a317c3ee90f8",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{22}$ and $a_{31}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 2, 3, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
